WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.571 --> 00:00:02.505
- [Narrator] These days,
to be an NBA champion,

00:00:02.505 --> 00:00:03.539
you're probably gonna need to be

00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:05.097
a top three-point shooting team,

00:00:05.097 --> 00:00:07.479
and who better to talk
threes then the NBA's

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:09.770
all-time leading three-point shooter, Ray Allen.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:11.938
- Hi, I'm Ray Allen.

00:00:11.938 --> 00:00:16.430
- [Narrator] Yeah, we
know, I already said that.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.705
Our story begins on February 7th, 1945,

00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:23.292
back when all shots were
worth a mere two points.

00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:25.099
That day, the Columbia University Lions

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:27.396
were picking on the Fordham
Rams in New York City

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:31.547
and the NCAA introduced the
world to the three-point shot.

00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:34.166
Basketball would never be the same again,

00:00:34.166 --> 00:00:38.367
once the NCAA permanently
adopted the triple in 1986.

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:40.065
The three point shot
mitigated the advantage

00:00:40.065 --> 00:00:43.146
taller, stronger players
were, well, born with.

00:00:43.146 --> 00:00:44.626
Sure, there were good small players,

00:00:44.626 --> 00:00:46.256
but incentivizing shots from distance

00:00:46.256 --> 00:00:50.275
leveled the playing field, or
should I say hardwood court.

00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:53.263
Fast forward 70 years and the
three-point shot is the shot

00:00:53.263 --> 00:00:54.629
and Ray Allen knows this.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:57.064
Ray is the most prolific three
point shooter in NBA history.

00:00:57.064 --> 00:00:58.432
(record scratch)
For now.

00:00:58.432 --> 00:00:59.763
- [Ray] What the hell?

00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:01.044
- [Narrator] And Ray, like
all basketball greats,

00:01:01.044 --> 00:01:02.383
is a student of the game.

00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:03.931
When did the three point
shot get introduced

00:01:03.931 --> 00:01:05.258
to professional basketball?

00:01:05.258 --> 00:01:08.235
- It all started with the
American Basketball Association,

00:01:08.235 --> 00:01:10.294
better known as the ABA.

00:01:10.294 --> 00:01:11.127
(whistle blowing)

00:01:11.127 --> 00:01:12.320
- [Narrator] Timeout.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.983
For all you non-basketball fans out there,

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.989
the NCAA is the National
Collegiate Athletic Association.

00:01:16.989 --> 00:01:18.319
That's college basketball.

00:01:18.319 --> 00:01:21.057
The ABA, a pro league,
was a sort of anti-NBA

00:01:21.057 --> 00:01:23.370
until it merged with the NBA in 1976.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:24.203
(whistle blowing)

00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:25.097
Fun.

00:01:25.097 --> 00:01:27.572
- [Ray] The ABA featured a
flashier version of basketball

00:01:27.572 --> 00:01:30.830
with a colorful ball,
dunks, and of course,

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:33.252
the three-point shot.

00:01:33.252 --> 00:01:35.265
- [Narrator] And for that, you can thank

00:01:35.265 --> 00:01:36.680
then NBA commissioner George Mikan,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:37.513
who wanted to --

00:01:37.513 --> 00:01:39.414
- Give the smaller
player a chance to score

00:01:39.414 --> 00:01:41.158
and open up the defense to make the game

00:01:41.158 --> 00:01:43.080
more enjoyable to fans.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.392
- [Narrator] Exactly.

00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:46.882
So the NBA adopted it pretty
much right away, right?

00:01:46.882 --> 00:01:47.824
- It took awhile.

00:01:47.824 --> 00:01:51.285
It wasn't until 1979, when
Boston Celtic Chris Ford,

00:01:51.285 --> 00:01:54.298
my first NBA coach, hit
the first three-point shot

00:01:54.298 --> 00:01:55.965
in an NBA game.

00:01:55.965 --> 00:01:58.103
- [Narrator] The first
of over 380,000 threes

00:01:58.103 --> 00:01:59.961
made in NBA history.

00:01:59.961 --> 00:02:02.104
We take a break from our
regularly schedule programming

00:02:02.104 --> 00:02:04.254
to bring you the art of
the three-point shot,

00:02:04.254 --> 00:02:06.155
by Mr. Walter Ray Allen, Jr.

00:02:06.155 --> 00:02:08.036
- The key to the three-
point shot is knowing

00:02:08.036 --> 00:02:10.143
where you are on the floor
before you get the ball.

00:02:10.143 --> 00:02:12.583
Nobody likes a long two.

00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:15.752
You need a great knee
bend, and gather yourself.

00:02:15.752 --> 00:02:20.107
Next, you need great lift,
straight up and down.

00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:22.239
And of course, to top it all off,

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:24.880
you need an immaculate follow through.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.090
It's so easy, anyone can do it.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.590
(ball bounce)

00:02:31.754 --> 00:02:33.016
Almost anyone.

00:02:33.016 --> 00:02:34.823
C'mon, little buddy, you got this.

00:02:34.823 --> 00:02:36.810
Get it in there.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.305
You know, your game might be horseshoes.

